  "answer_core": "I would not grant Marcus 15% equity, a CTO title, and a board seat on Friday. Those are irreversible governance and ownership commitments, and you do not yet have evidence that the partnership reduces concentration risk rather than increasing it.\n\nSeveral dependencies currently run through him at once: the platform prototype, technical delivery, Jake and Lina, client confidence, and part of the exit story. If you grant the full package now, you price contribution and concentration risk before knowing whether he stabilizes or destabilizes those dependencies. You also risk turning informal promises into public commitments before written evidence and board process exist.\n\nSo Friday should be a direct, structured conversation about a serious staged partnership—not a flat rejection and not a vague \"later\" that reads as avoidance. A vague delay can trigger exactly what you want to prevent: Marcus disengaging, pulling others with him, slowing the platform, or destabilizing client continuity.\n\nYour position should be that there may be a path to major upside and leadership for him, but not before proof. Propose a short, defined sprint that tests whether he can deliver the key platform work, whether that work matters for revenue, whether team continuity improves rather than worsens, whether knowledge transfer happens, whether his governance behavior fits what the company needs, and whether he strengthens the company beyond himself. Spell out what specific outcomes in that window would unlock the next stage of equity, title, and governance conversations. Keep intermediate structures alive, but do not let the discussion turn into a catalog of instruments.\n\nUse exit math only as false-precision caution. Until buyer assumptions, platform value, and departure scenarios are tested, the numbers should not carry the decision.\n\nGo in ready to say you want to explore a serious path, but you cannot make irreversible commitments before proving what the partnership does to the company.",
